var s={schema:"fieldwork/caps/1",note:`Every magnitude constant that can refuse a practitioner's work, declared where it can be enumerated rather than met one at a time. The practitioner, 2026-10-22, after a week of them: "Where are these caps coming from? It seems from the outset of trying to use this widget there are caps left and right." and "I have wasted 2 days with these caps." Six fired in sequence over two days -- a graph-limit ceiling that equalled its own default, a pre-download count that counted ways and not elements, a junction cap derived from an isochrone measurement, a 30,000-vertex geometry cap, a 100,000-character provenance cap, and a 100 km edge cap -- and each was discovered only after the cost of the previous one had been paid. Blockers and directions have had a ledger, a clock and a validator since 2026-10-12. Caps had a prose inventory (experiment 125) that listed some of them and missed the rest.

Each cap here is also an individual in ontology/fieldwork.ttl, so a receipt can say which ones governed a run and at what value. A number that refuses work and cannot be queried is a number nobody can argue with.`,basisVocabulary:{measured:"Taken from a measurement of the real thing, recorded with what was measured.",derived:"Computed from a measurement, with any headroom stated.",undderived:"Chosen. Says so, and carries a liftTest that would replace it with a measurement."},watchedFiles:["src/catchments.ts","src/study-area.ts","src/network-preview.ts","src/street-network.ts","src/raster.ts","src/catchment-map.ts","src/evidence.ts","src/data-table.ts","src/chart-output.ts","src/raster-limits.ts","src/point-layers.ts"],measuredAt:"2026-11-05",caps:[{id:"storage-quota",constant:"STORAGE_QUOTA_BYTES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:storage-quota",value:5226496,unit:"bytes",basis:"measured",evidence:"localStorage accepted 5,226,496 bytes in both engines, probed 2026-10-19. docs/experiments/121-storage-beyond-local-storage.md.",refuses:"A workflow whose saved form does not fit the browser's small store.",liftTest:"Workflows move to the large store, at which point this governs nothing.",kind:"platform"},{id:"graph-limit-default",constant:"NETWORK_NODE_LIMIT",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:graph-limit-default",value:5e4,unit:"junctions",basis:"derived",evidence:"Bunia normalizes to 42,796 junctions and Buvuma Island to 11,359, measured 2026-10-20; the default must not refuse a town.",refuses:"Nothing by itself. It is the default value of a knob a practitioner sets.",liftTest:"Not a cap to lift: a default to re-derive when a larger real area is measured.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"graph-limit-ceiling",constant:"NETWORK_NODE_LIMIT_MAX",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:graph-limit-ceiling",value:1e6,unit:"junctions",basis:"undderived",evidence:"Chosen 2026-10-21 to sit past what a public Overpass instance will serve in one request. It was 50,000 -- the default itself -- so the knob could be lowered and never raised, and the byte cap derived from it could never be raised either. That is the cap that cost two days.",refuses:"A graph limit a practitioner tries to set above it.",liftTest:"A measured response size at which a browser actually fails, replacing a chosen number with a met one.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"download-bytes-per-junction",constant:"BYTES_PER_NODE",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:download-bytes-per-junction",value:600,unit:"bytes per junction",basis:"derived",evidence:"270 and 272 bytes per junction measured on two extents 2026-10-13, carried at roughly double as headroom. A province measured 163 on 2026-10-21, so the padding is nearly fourfold at that scale.",refuses:"Nothing directly; it sizes the download byte cap from the graph limit.",liftTest:"Re-derive from province-scale measurements rather than neighbourhood ones.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"predicted-bytes-per-element",constant:"BYTES_PER_FEATURE",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:predicted-bytes-per-element",value:150,unit:"bytes per element",basis:"measured",evidence:"1.42 MB over 9,458 elements is 150 B; 7.39 MB over 50,791 is 145 B. Unpadded, because it predicts a response rather than bounding one.",refuses:"A download whose predicted size, from the service's own count, exceeds the byte cap.",liftTest:"Nothing to lift: it is a measurement used to refuse before bytes are spent, which is the cheap place to refuse.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"features-per-junction",constant:"FEATURES_PER_NODE",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:features-per-junction",value:4,unit:"features per junction",basis:"derived",evidence:"1.80 and 1.87 features per junction measured 2026-10-13, carried at roughly double.",refuses:"Nothing directly; it sizes the feature cap from the graph limit.",liftTest:"Re-derive at province scale.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"segments-per-junction",constant:"SEGMENTS_PER_NODE",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:segments-per-junction",value:2.5,unit:"directed segments per junction",basis:"derived",evidence:"2.19 measured on real street data (11,497 over 5,249); 2.04 measured on a province 2026-10-21.",refuses:"Nothing now. It sized a graph refusal that was removed on 2026-10-21.",liftTest:"Not a cap any more; kept because the pair is still reported.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"download-floor-bytes",constant:"DOWNLOAD_FLOOR_BYTES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:download-floor-bytes",value:16e6,unit:"bytes",basis:"derived",evidence:"Twice the largest extent this project had measured (7.39 MB), so the floor cannot refuse an example it ships.",refuses:"Nothing: it is a floor under the byte cap, not a ceiling over it.",liftTest:"Not applicable.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"download-floor-features",constant:"DOWNLOAD_FLOOR_FEATURES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:download-floor-features",value:12e4,unit:"features",basis:"derived",evidence:"Twice the 50,791 features of that same extent.",refuses:"Nothing: a floor.",liftTest:"Not applicable.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"edge-length",constant:"MAX_EDGE_METRES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:edge-length",value:1e6,unit:"metres",basis:"undderived",evidence:"Raised from 100,000 on 2026-10-22, when 2 of 314,955 segments in a province's driving network were over it, the longest 100,723 m. A collapsed chain between junctions in rural Ituri is genuinely that long. 1,000 km is kept as a unit check: a length in degrees, or kilometres read as metres, lands orders of magnitude away.",refuses:"A single street segment longer than 1,000 km.",liftTest:"A unit declared on the field, at which point the magnitude check has nothing left to catch.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"isochrone-measured-junctions",constant:"ISOCHRONE_MEASURED_NODES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:isochrone-measured-junctions",value:2e3,unit:"junctions",basis:"measured",evidence:"A 1 km square of central London normalizes to 5,249 junctions and exhausted a 4 GB heap during isochrone construction, 2026-10-13. The threshold sits below what failed.",refuses:"Nothing. Above it the isochrone step warns, and its own worker timeout reports what happened.",liftTest:"An isochrone implementation measured past it -- the street engine drew one over 1,152,974 junctions in 0.14 s on 2026-10-22, in WebAssembly and not in this code.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"preview-segments",constant:"PREVIEW_SEGMENT_LIMIT",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:preview-segments",value:2e4,unit:"segments drawn",basis:"derived",evidence:"A 292-pixel Inspector figure. A province's 3,755,832 segments would be tens of megabytes of path data and thousands of strokes per pixel.",refuses:"Nothing. Beyond it the picture is an even sample and the caption says so.",liftTest:"Not applicable: a limit on a drawing, not on data.",kind:"artefact"},{id:"interactive-segments",constant:"INTERACTIVE_SEGMENT_LIMIT",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:interactive-segments",value:2e5,unit:"segments drawn",kind:"artefact",basis:"measured",evidence:"Measured 2026-10-22 in a Chromium tab with Leaflet's canvas renderer and straight two-point segments: 100,000 added in 76 ms and zoomed in 29 ms, 250,000 zoomed in 185 ms, 500,000 in 274 ms and 1,011,401 in 824 ms. Cost climbs steeply past a quarter of a million because every pan and zoom reprojects every segment, and a real edge carries geometry which multiplies it further. Ituri arrived the same day with 2,022,803 directed segments and the interactive map tried to draw all of them.",refuses:"Nothing. Beyond it the map draws an even sample and the layer control says how many of how many.",liftTest:"Not applicable: a limit on a drawing. The graph is untouched -- every junction is routed over, measured and exported."},{id:"raster-window-cells",constant:"MAX_RASTER_CELLS",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:raster-window-cells",value:5189e5,unit:"cells",kind:"discovery",basis:"measured",evidence:"Measured 2026-11-17 in headless Chromium on a Mac: WorldPop R2025A 2026 over DR Congo, a study area across the whole file, 22,935 x 22,621 = 518,812,635 cells of 4 bytes (2.07 GB), through Raster input's own dialog, then the project opened afresh (which runs it) and run again: Apply 75 s, the tab at 2.63 GB at its peak; the run on opening 2.66 GB; the run again 3.01 GB. Set 518,900,000. What let it through, the same day: the window read straight into the place its saved file keeps the cells, so reading and saving are one copy, not two; the digest of a large window taken over its bytes, not a copy; the stored parts read back one at a time; the header of a kept window read from its bytes, not through a Blob; and the last run's raster let go when the next run starts. Before those, DR Congo was read in 15 s and the tab died saving it at about 6 GB, and Nigeria (166,369,302 cells) ran at 2.1 to 2.3 GB for Apply and 3.0 to 3.6 GB for a run pressed again; after, 1.60 GB and 1.30 GB. Earlier limits: 166,400,000 (Nigeria, 2026-11-17); 123,300,000 (GHS-POP over Botswana, 2026-11-13, 123,276,912 cells of 8 bytes, tests/population-models-from-scratch.browser.mjs); 123,200,000 (WorldPop Botswana, 2026-11-12); 16,777,216 (2026-10-22).",refuses:"A study area whose window on a raster holds more than 518.9 million cells. DR Congo whole at 100 m from a 4-byte source fits. An 8-byte source reaches the decoding limit (2.1 GB) first, at about 265 million cells.",liftTest:"Not yet tried on Windows: Botswana's window ran in Edge there; Nigeria's and DR Congo's are to be tried, and a tab there may hold less than on a Mac. Measure a larger window the same way, in Chromium and WebKit. GHS-POP over Nigeria, 8 bytes a cell and 1.33 GB, is admitted and has not been run. A window of more than 2^31 bytes would pass the largest single array some browsers allow; the decoding limit sits just under it."},{id:"json-editor-elements",constant:"JSON_EDITOR_ELEMENT_LIMIT",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:json-editor-elements",value:2e4,unit:"nodes plus directed edges",basis:"measured",evidence:"Measured 2026-10-22 on the Ituri extract in the Inspector: 113,485 nodes and 289,080 directed edges serialise to 41.8 MB pretty-printed and 59.7 MB after escaping, about 203 MB of UTF-16 held per render, in a collapsed <details> that showed none of it. Chrome killed the renderer. 20,000 elements is about 1.9 MB, the limit this same control already states for upload.",refuses:"Nothing about the network. Above it the hand-edit textarea is not offered and the panel says so; the file input stays and the graph is still held, drawn, run and exported.",liftTest:"Not applicable: a limit on a text box, not on data.",kind:"artefact"},{id:"download-measured-junctions",constant:"NETWORK_MEASURED_NODES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:download-measured-junctions",value:27181,unit:"junctions",basis:"measured",evidence:"The largest graph this project has downloaded from the service, parsed and drawn: 27,181 junctions from a 7.39 MB response, 2026-10-13.",refuses:"Nothing. Above it the download warns that nothing larger has been tried.",liftTest:"A larger response downloaded, parsed and drawn, which replaces the figure.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"extract-measured-junctions",constant:"EXTRACT_MEASURED_NODES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:extract-measured-junctions",value:122061,unit:"junctions",basis:"measured",evidence:"A province's driving network read from a 416 MB published extract and taken into this application's structures, 2026-10-22. Held, not yet drawn, routed over or snapped to.",refuses:"Nothing. It is what the extract control reports having been measured to.",liftTest:"A larger extract-built graph drawn and routed over, which replaces the figure.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"buffer-batch",constant:"BUFFER_BATCH",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:buffer-batch",value:25,unit:"geometries per batch",basis:"undderived",evidence:"Chosen so buffering many geometries yields to the event loop rather than blocking it. No measurement recorded.",refuses:"Nothing. It is a batch size, and a smaller one is only slower.",liftTest:"A measurement of frame time against batch size, which would replace the choice with a number.",kind:"artefact"},{id:"preview-width",constant:"PREVIEW_WIDTH",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:preview-width",value:252,unit:"pixels",basis:"derived",evidence:"The Inspector panel is 292 pixels wide; the figure is that less its padding.",refuses:"Nothing. It is the width of a drawing.",liftTest:"Not applicable: a panel dimension, not a limit on data.",kind:"artefact"},{id:"preview-height",constant:"PREVIEW_HEIGHT",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:preview-height",value:168,unit:"pixels",basis:"derived",evidence:"Two thirds of the figure's width, so a square extent is not stretched.",refuses:"Nothing. It is the height of a drawing.",liftTest:"Not applicable: a panel dimension, not a limit on data.",kind:"artefact"},{id:"corridor-buckets",constant:"D3_BUCKETS",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:corridor-buckets",value:6,unit:"buckets",basis:"derived",evidence:"How finely a partial edge's corridor is approximated. Measured 2026-10-14 against an exact construction: 161,044 m2 against 160,785, a symmetric difference of 0.16%, and not purely conservative -- which the code states rather than assumes.",refuses:"Nothing. It trades area accuracy for work, and the error is measured.",liftTest:"Not applicable: an approximation parameter with its error stated, not a limit on data.",kind:"artefact"},{id:"attempt-seconds",constant:"ATTEMPT_MS",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:attempt-seconds",value:18e4,unit:"milliseconds",kind:"discovery",basis:"derived",evidence:"A RESPONSE budget, and only that since 2026-10-22. Raised 2026-10-19 from 20,000 after a one-line probe to overpass-api.de took 14.2, 27.3 and 26.3 seconds while answering 200 every time -- which showed 20 seconds was too short and nothing more. The replacement was borrowed from OSMnx, where 180 seconds is passed to Python requests, and there it is not a limit on a download at all: requests raises only when no bytes have arrived for that long. Imported here as a total elapsed deadline it meant something else, and aborted a transfer that was working.",refuses:"A service that has not begun to answer within three minutes. It no longer refuses a transfer that is arriving; STALL_MS governs that, which is what the borrowed number always meant.",liftTest:"A measured time at which a response of a given size actually arrives, so the budget follows the size rather than a borrowed default."},{id:"stall-seconds",constant:"STALL_MS",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:stall-seconds",value:9e4,unit:"milliseconds",kind:"artefact",basis:"derived",evidence:`When the panel says in so many words that the service has gone quiet. It was 180,000 -- inherited from the response budget it replaced -- which meant a stalled transfer showed its last byte count for three minutes and looked exactly like one still arriving. Ninety seconds is the practitioner's, 2026-10-22: "180s may be too long to wait to say it's stalled. 90s, time to give feedback." The faster half of the feedback is the transfer rate, said every second, which falls on its own the moment chunks stop.`,refuses:"Nothing. It is when a sentence appears. A download that has begun to arrive is ended only by the practitioner pressing Stop.",liftTest:"Not applicable: a threshold for a status line, not a limit on data."},{id:"rate-window",constant:"RATE_WINDOW_MS",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:rate-window",value:5e3,unit:"milliseconds",kind:"artefact",basis:"derived",evidence:`How much history the transfer rate is averaged over, so the number is not noise between chunks and still falls while someone is watching it. The practitioner, 2026-10-22: "Feedback could also be bytes/sec download speed. If the user sees it going down, that's immediate feedback."`,refuses:"Nothing. It is the width of an average shown in a status line.",liftTest:"Not applicable: a display window, not a limit on data."},{id:"total-seconds",constant:"TOTAL_MS",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:total-seconds",value:3e5,unit:"milliseconds",kind:"discovery",basis:"derived",evidence:"Five minutes across the probe, the count, the failover attempts and any wait for a free slot. It is consulted between attempts and to bound a wait, never during a transfer: a stream that is delivering is governed by STALL_MS alone.",refuses:"A download whose attempts together run past five minutes before a service begins to answer.",liftTest:"The same measurement: a budget derived from response size rather than chosen."},{id:"status-timeout",constant:"STATUS_MS",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:status-timeout",value:12e3,unit:"milliseconds",kind:"artefact",basis:"undderived",evidence:"How long this client waits for an instance's /api/status before calling it unreachable for the purposes of choosing one. Chosen.",refuses:"Nothing a practitioner asked for: a slow status read only removes an instance from the preference order.",liftTest:"Not applicable: it bounds this client's own impatience, not what can be found."},{id:"bundle-bytes",constant:"MAX_BUNDLE_BYTES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:bundle-bytes",value:5e8,unit:"bytes",kind:"discovery",basis:"measured",evidence:"The largest project or bundle that may be read back in. It was 20,000,000, carrying no evidence, and the practitioner met it moving their own work from Safari to Chrome after Chrome would no longer open it. Raised to 500,000,000 on 2026-10-23 at their direction and measured rather than inferred: a project document of 2.6 million junctions serialises to 505,485,882 characters and parses in 3.3 seconds, and a 465 MB indented document was read and parsed in a Chromium tab in 1.1 seconds. V8 refuses a string past 536,870,888 characters -- asked directly, not assumed -- so a file at this limit sits about six per cent under the ceiling it would meet first, and the practitioner's own 472 MB workflow about twelve. Exports are compressed since 2026-10-22 and compact since 2026-10-23, so a file near this limit is an older uncompressed one.",refuses:'A project or bundle larger than 500 MB, or a compressed one that expands past it. The margin to the string ceiling above is small, and the failure there is "Invalid string length".',liftTest:"The rest of the import, which is unmeasured: validating the document and writing its payloads to the store. The better answer is a backup that does not carry data which can be downloaded again -- recorded as the direction a-backup-carries-the-work-not-the-data."},{id:"pdf-bytes",constant:"MAX_PDF_BYTES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:pdf-bytes",value:5e6,unit:"bytes",kind:"artefact",basis:"derived",evidence:"One cited PDF attached to a workflow as evidence. A reference document, not data under analysis.",refuses:"A single attached PDF over 5 MB.",liftTest:"A measured distribution of the documents practitioners actually cite."},{id:"workflow-pdf-bytes",constant:"MAX_WORKFLOW_PDF_BYTES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:workflow-pdf-bytes",value:1e7,unit:"bytes",kind:"artefact",basis:"derived",evidence:"Every cited PDF in one workflow together, so a workflow's evidence stays something a reader can receive.",refuses:"A workflow whose attached PDFs exceed 10 MB in total.",liftTest:"The same measurement as pdf-bytes, taken across a workflow."},{id:"table-rows",constant:"MAX_TABLE_ROWS",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:table-rows",value:25e4,unit:"rows",basis:"measured",evidence:"Measured 2026-11-04 in Chromium on a build with every row ceiling lifted: at 250,000 rows import, save, reload, a date range, panel charts, a Poisson regression with strata and HAC, and export all completed (slowest: the panel chart, 26-32 s). At 500,000 the page stopped answering clicks for over 15 s during the panel chart and the regression; at 1,000,000 the tab crashed. The regression kernel's buffers (kernel/src/glm.rs MAX_ROWS) are set to the same number. docs/experiments/175-how-large-a-table.md.",refuses:"A table of more rows, or a wide file whose melted values exceed it.",liftTest:"Measure again past 250,000 with the steps that slowed first (the panel chart, the page holding every row) changed; the cap follows the first step that fails.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"table-file-bytes",constant:"MAX_TABLE_FILE_BYTES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:table-file-bytes",value:4e7,unit:"bytes",basis:"measured",evidence:"Measured 2026-11-04 in Chromium: a 42.4 MB, 1,000,000-row CSV was read and previewed in 10.4 s. Rows, not bytes, are where the application stopped coping, so the file limit sits below the largest file read. Raised from 5 MB, which had been a second, unrecorded ceiling inside the CSV reader as well as the dialog. docs/experiments/175-how-large-a-table.md.",refuses:"A table file larger than 40 MB.",liftTest:"Read a larger file with many columns and few rows; if it is read whole and quickly, the file limit can follow the row limit alone.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"panel-points",constant:"PANEL_POINT_LIMIT",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:panel-points",value:25e4,unit:"points",basis:"measured",evidence:"Measured 2026-11-04 in Chromium: 250,000 points in seven panels drew in 26-32 s, 100,000 in 10 s, 25,571 in 2.7 s. At 500,000 the page stopped answering. Equal to the table limit, so a table that can be read can be drawn. docs/experiments/175-how-large-a-table.md.",refuses:"A panel chart of more points than that.",liftTest:"Whether 30 s for a chart is read as the application failing or the data being large is the practitioner observation in the experiment; drawing fewer marks per pixel would change the measurement.",kind:"discovery"},{id:"raster-decode-bytes",constant:"MAX_DECODE_BYTES",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:raster-decode-bytes",value:2124e6,unit:"bytes of TIFF blocks",kind:"discovery",basis:"measured",evidence:"Measured 2026-11-17: WorldPop R2025A 2026's 512 by 512 tiles over DR Congo, 4-byte cells, 2,123,366,400 bytes of blocks, read and kept through Raster input in a Chromium tab, the tab at 2.63 GB at its peak. Set 2,124,000,000. Before that, 2026-11-13: GHS-POP R2023A's tiles over Botswana, 1.06 GB of 8-byte blocks, read by range from the JRC's copy, set 1,100,000,000; 2026-10-22: Botswana's WorldPop 2025 raster, 493 MB of blocks, set 512,000,000.",refuses:"A read whose intersecting blocks hold more than 2.1 GB.",liftTest:"Read a larger raster's window in a Chromium tab and record the time and the memory."},{id:"workflow-file-attachment-chars",constant:"MAX_WORKFLOW_FILE_ATTACHMENT_CHARS",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:workflow-file-attachment-chars",value:236e6,unit:"base64 characters",kind:"artefact",basis:"measured",evidence:"Measured 2026-11-12 in a Chromium tab at its default heap: a workflow file carrying a 134 MB raster (178.9M characters) was written in 2.1 s and one of 177 MB (236.3M) in 2.8 s; one of 220 MB crashed the tab both times it was tried, as did one of 268 MB. The text is held several times over while it is written: the file in base64, the document, and its compression.",refuses:"Nothing: a workflow whose files would be larger is backed up with \u201CThis workflow, encrypted\u201D, which carries them as they are, and the message says so.",liftTest:"Write the workflow file as a stream rather than one text, then measure again past 220 MB."},{id:"point-records",constant:"MAX_POINT_RECORDS",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:point-records",value:25e4,unit:"point records",kind:"discovery",basis:"measured",evidence:"Measured 2026-11-19 in Chromium on the development Mac, tests/measure-point-count.browser.mjs (MEASURE_POINTS, MEASURE_VIA=file): points spread over about a degree around Kinshasa, inside a drawn study area, run on their own to a static map, an interactive map and a table, each timed from Run to the figure on screen. At 250,000 the static map ran in 18 s and drew in 19 s, and the table ran in 10 s and drew in 5.5 s; the page answered a click within 2.5 s after each. Equal to the table limit, so a table of points that can be read can be drawn. Before, 2,000 combined across a map's point layers and on every point import, chosen.",refuses:"Point data of more than 250,000 records, from a file, pins, generated points or reprojection; a map or table given more than that.",liftTest:"Measure 500,000 points the same way. A table of more than 250,000 rows cannot be made, so the file route is the one to try; the interactive map has its own, lower limit."},{id:"interactive-marks",constant:"INTERACTIVE_MARKS_LIMIT",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:interactive-marks",value:1e5,unit:"marks of a shape other than a circle",kind:"artefact",basis:"measured",evidence:"Measured 2026-11-19 in Chromium on the development Mac, tests/measure-point-count.browser.mjs (MEASURE_POINTS, MEASURE_VIA=file): points spread over about a degree around Kinshasa, inside a drawn study area, run on their own to a static map, an interactive map and a table, each timed from Run to the figure on screen. Large layers drawn by MapLibre GL: 10,000 marks in 1.5 s, 50,000 in 5.8 s, 100,000 in 11 s. At 150,000 and 200,000 the tab crashed while MapLibre's source prepared the points to zoom 18; cut at zoom 12, nothing was drawn, the layout of an icon per mark never finishing. Circles drawn with MapLibre's circle layer: 150,000 in 13 s, 250,000 in 24 s, so circles have no limit of their own below the record limit. Before the graphics card drew them, one Leaflet marker each: 10,000 in 21 s, 50,000 in 155 s.",refuses:"Nothing. Past it, marks of other shapes are drawn as circles in their colour, and the map says so; the static plot draws their shapes.",liftTest:"Draw the other shapes another way than an icon per mark (a shape drawn in the shader, as the circle layer draws circles) and measure again."},{id:"merged-marks",constant:"MERGED_MARKS_FROM",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:merged-marks",value:2e3,unit:"marks in a layer",kind:"artefact",basis:"derived",evidence:"One marker or SVG element per mark drew 2,000 in under 2 s and 10,000 in 8 s (static) and 21 s (interactive), 2026-11-19. Over 2,000, a layer is one path per style on the static figure and one layer on the graphics card on the interactive map: 50,000 then took 2.7 s and 5.8 s.",refuses:"Nothing. Over it, a layer's marks are not each focusable or named.",liftTest:"Not a cap to lift: where one element per mark stops being worth its cost to a reader is a practitioner observation."},{id:"label-ceiling",constant:"LABEL_CEILING",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:label-ceiling",value:24,unit:"marks named",kind:"artefact",basis:"undderived",evidence:"Chosen 2026-10-31: four intakes on the Thames want their names printed; 250 death locations do not.",refuses:"Nothing. A layer over it is drawn unnamed and Configure says why.",liftTest:"A figure whose reader needs more than 24 names on one layer."},{id:"point-layers",constant:"MAX_POINT_LAYERS",iri:"urn:fieldwork:cap:point-layers",value:8,unit:"point layers per step",kind:"artefact",basis:"undderived",evidence:"Chosen; no record says why 8. Since 2026-11-19 it is the one number every point port follows.",refuses:"A ninth point layer on a map, a table or Deduplicate points. Points from more sources can be stacked with Append rows first.",liftTest:"A map that needs a ninth layer kept separate in its key."}],kindVocabulary:{artefact:"Bounds something this application makes and controls: a 292-pixel drawing, a batch size, how finely a shape is approximated. Reasonable by construction -- the thing it bounds is ours, and the practitioner loses nothing they came to find.",platform:"A limit the environment imposes, which this application measures and reports rather than chooses. The remedy is to name it, never to pretend it is not there.",discovery:'Bounds what a practitioner can find out. These are the dangerous ones. The practitioner, 2026-10-22: "There are reasonable caps and unreasonable ones... The others are unreasonable because they are imposing limits on things we have yet and need to discover (the DE angle)." A number chosen here decides the answer to a question nobody has asked yet.'},chosenDiscoveryCeiling:10,ceilingRule:'It may be lowered freely and may never be raised. Widened 2026-10-22 from chosen numbers alone to every discovery cap whose basis is not a measurement. The practitioner, on finding a borrowed 180 seconds stopping a working download: "That is the definition of an unreasonable cap, which I thought we know how to classify already." The classification was right -- attempt-seconds was declared discovery, derived, with a liftTest naming the measurement that would replace it -- and it bound nothing, because only undderived caps were counted. A derived number is still a number nobody measured here.'};var o=s.caps,r=i=>o.find(e=>e.id===i);function c(i={}){let e=["graph-limit-ceiling","download-bytes-per-junction","predicted-bytes-per-element","features-per-junction","edge-length","download-floor-bytes","download-floor-features"].map(r).filter(a=>!!a).map(a=>({cap:a,inForce:a.value})),t=r("graph-limit-default");return t&&e.unshift({cap:t,inForce:i.maxNodes??t.value}),e}function h(i,e){return e.map(({cap:t,inForce:a})=>`<${i}> fw:governedBy <${t.iri}>.
<${t.iri}> fw:capValueInForce ${a}.
`).join("")}export{o as a,r as b,c,h as d};
